\begin_layout Standard
There are several definitions for the ray launching angles used in ECCD.
 The most common definitions are listed here.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
for the vertical angle :
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\:\left[-\pi;\pi\right]$
\end_inset

 : angle between the poloidal projection of the ray and the horizontal plane.
 Refered to as the poloidal angle in [pra08].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\alpha}\:\left[-\pi/2;\pi/2\right]$
\end_inset

 : angle between the ray and the horizontal plane.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\:\left[0;\pi\right]$
\end_inset

 : angle between the ray and the Z axis [LUKE].
 Refered to as the polar angle in [pra08].
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
for the horizontal angle :
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\:\left[-\pi/2;\pi/2\right]$
\end_inset

 : angle between the ray and the poloidal plane.
 Refered to as the toroidal angle in [pra08].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}\:\left[-\pi;\pi\right]$
\end_inset

 : angle between the horizontal projection of the ray and the poloidal plane.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\phi\:\left[0;2\pi\right]$
\end_inset

 : angle between the ray and the R axis [LUKE].
 Refered to as the azimuthal angle in [pra08].
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
These angles are reported on Fig.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "figure"

\end_inset

.
 Note that these definitions are valid both in the case of low-field side
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\alpha\right|<\pi/2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\tilde{\beta}\right|<\pi/2$
\end_inset

) and high-field side (
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\alpha\right|>\pi/2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\tilde{\beta}\right|>\pi/2$
\end_inset

) launching.
 Note that the sign convention is that the angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 are positive as indicated by arrows on the figure.
 The convention for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 depends upon the direction of the toroidal angle.
 The angles 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 are defined uniquely with respect to R and Z.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The angles 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 are similar to the angles 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 They are simply related through
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\begin{aligned}\theta & =\frac{\pi}{2}-\tilde{\alpha}\\
\phi & =\pi+\tilde{\beta}\end{aligned}
}\label{tpab}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relations between 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 are expressed by four geometrical relations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\sin\beta & = & \cos\tilde{\alpha}\sin\tilde{\beta}\label{rel1}\\
\tan\beta & = & \cos\alpha\tan\tilde{\beta}\label{rel2}\\
\sin\tilde{\alpha} & = & \cos\beta\sin\alpha\label{rel3}\\
\tan\tilde{\alpha} & = & \cos\tilde{\beta}\tan\alpha\label{rel4}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The transformation from 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha,\beta)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{\alpha},\tilde{\beta})$
\end_inset

 coordinates is derived from (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "rel3"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "rel2"

\end_inset

)
\backslash
_
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\begin{aligned}\tilde{\alpha} & =\arcsin\left(\cos\beta\sin\alpha\right)\\
\tilde{\beta} & =\arctan\left(\frac{\tan\beta}{\cos\alpha}\right)+\pi\textrm{sign}(\beta)\left[\left|\alpha\right|>\frac{\pi}{2}\right]\end{aligned}
}\label{abtab}\end{equation}

\end_inset

such that the transformation from 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha,\beta)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{\alpha},\tilde{\beta})$
\end_inset

 coordinates is (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tpab"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "abtab"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\boxed{\theta=\arccos\left(\cos\beta\sin\alpha\right)}\label{thetaab}\\
\phi=\pi+\arctan\left(\frac{\tan\beta}{\cos\alpha}\right)+\pi\textrm{sign}(\beta)\left[\left|\alpha\right|>\frac{\pi}{2}\right]\label{phiab}\end{align}

\end_inset

Note that instead of (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "v"

\end_inset

) we can use (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "rel1"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tpab"

\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\phi=\pi+\arcsin\left(\frac{\sin\beta}{\sin\theta}\right)+\pi\textrm{sign}(\beta)\left[\left|\alpha\right|>\frac{\pi}{2}\right]}\label{phitb}\end{equation}

\end_inset

which avoids the numerical errors that ay arise from (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "v"

\end_inset

).
 The expression (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "g"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "phitb"

\end_inset

) were derived in [pra08] for the case of low-field side launching only.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The inverse relation could also be useful.
 We can transform from 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{\alpha},\tilde{\beta})$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha,\beta)$
\end_inset

 according to the relations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\alpha=\arctan\left(\frac{\tan\tilde{\alpha}}{\cos\tilde{\beta}}\right)+\pi\textrm{sign}(\tilde{\alpha})\left[\left|\tilde{\beta}\right|>\frac{\pi}{2}\right]\\
\boxed{\beta=\arcsin\left(\cos\tilde{\alpha}\sin\tilde{\beta}\right)}\end{align*}

\end_inset

Again, we may use instead
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\alpha=\arcsin\left(\frac{\sin\tilde{\alpha}}{\cos\beta}\right)+\pi\textrm{sign}(\tilde{\alpha})\left[\left|\tilde{\beta}\right|>\frac{\pi}{2}\right]}\label{aatb}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition, it is possible to derive the following cross-transformations
 : with prior knowledge of LFS or HFS launching, we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{\alpha},\beta)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha,\tilde{\beta})$
\end_inset

 by using (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "aatb"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "rel1"

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\alpha & =\arcsin\left(\frac{\sin\tilde{\alpha}}{\cos\beta}\right)+\pi\textrm{sign}(\tilde{\alpha})\left[\textrm{HFS}\right]\\
\tilde{\beta} & =\arcsin\left(\frac{\sin\beta}{\cos\tilde{\alpha}}\right)+\pi\textrm{sign}(\beta)\left[\textrm{HFS}\right]\end{align*}

\end_inset

We can also derive 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha,\tilde{\beta})$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{\alpha},\beta)$
\end_inset

 from (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "rel4"

\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\tilde{\alpha} & =\arctan\left(\cos\tilde{\beta}\tan\alpha\right)\\
\beta & =\arctan\left(\cos\alpha\tan\tilde{\beta}\right)\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the launching point is in free space the index of refraction 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{n}$
\end_inset

 is parallel to the ray direction and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert \mathbf{n}\right\Vert =1$
\end_inset

.
 The parallel index of refraction is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
n_{\Vert}\equiv\mathbf{n}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{b}}=\frac{n_{R}\cdot B_{R}+n_{Z}\cdot B_{Z}+n_{\phi}\cdot B_{\phi}}{\left\Vert \mathbf{B}\right\Vert }\]

\end_inset

where we have the relations
\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{alignedat}{2}n_{R} & =-\cos\alpha\cos\beta & =-\cos\tilde{\beta}\cos\tilde{\alpha} & =\cos\phi\sin\theta\\
n_{Z} & =\sin\alpha\cos\beta & =\sin\tilde{\alpha} & =\cos\theta\\
n_{\phi} & =\sin\beta & =\sin\tilde{\beta}\cos & =-\sin\phi\sin\theta\end{alignedat}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

